-- postpartum week 129: Who decides what at mealtimes? The division of responsibility in feeding
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   129,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Who decides what at mealtimes? The division of responsibility in feeding',
   '"You decide what, when and where your toddler eats — your toddler decides whether and how much. Both jobs matter, and mixing them up is often where mealtime battles start."',
   'The division of responsibility in feeding is a widely used framework, developed by dietitian and family therapist Ellyn Satter, that splits mealtime jobs between parent and child. From toddlerhood onward, the parent decides what food is offered, when meals and snacks happen, and where eating takes place. The child decides whether to eat what''s offered, and how much. AAP''s own parent-facing guidance independently states a very similar split, and WHO''s guideline on feeding young children describes ''responsive feeding'' — supporting a child''s ability to eat according to their own hunger and fullness cues — as a recommended practice. Research on how parents handle feeding is genuinely mixed on some points and more settled on others, and this week tries to represent that honestly rather than promise a single technique will end every mealtime struggle. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has a diagnosed feeding disorder, avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID), a documented growth or weight concern, or a significant sensory or developmental feeding difficulty: follow your child''s healthcare team''s individualised guidance this week, rather than the general approach described here — this is covered further on Day 5. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s menu doesn''t change specially for this topic — it''s the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses, and it already reflects the division of responsibility in a small way. Choosing between option A or B is your job, based on what your family enjoys and has on hand. Once the food is offered, your toddler decides whether to eat and how much to eat, without pressure. If a named fruit — including dragon fruit or persimmon — isn''t available or affordable where you live, substitute a familiar, safely prepared seasonal fruit such as banana, papaya, guava or orange instead. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What the division of responsibility actually means", "notes": "The division of responsibility in feeding, developed by dietitian Ellyn Satter, splits mealtime jobs in two. From toddlerhood onward, the parent is responsible for what food is offered, when meals and snacks happen, and where eating takes place — choosing food the family enjoys, keeping meals and snacks at fairly regular times, and choosing where the family eats together. The child is responsible for whether to eat what''s offered, and how much. This does not exclude the child from family food choices. A caregiver can consider the child''s preferences, involve them in simple choices and regularly include at least one familiar food, while still deciding what balanced options are available. This is Ellyn Satter''s own framework, built from decades of clinical and family-therapy experience rather than a single peer-reviewed trial — widely respected and used by many paediatric professionals, but described here as a framework, not a settled clinical guideline.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with finely grated cucumber mixed through, served with plain curd", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with finely grated cucumber mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked parwal (pointed gourd), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked parwal (pointed gourd), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe chikoo (sapota), peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground cashew mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if cashew has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with moong dal and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What the research says — and what it doesn''t", "notes": "Research on parental feeding practices offers a genuinely mixed picture, and this week tries not to flatten it. Restricting a child''s access to a food is the more consistently studied finding, associated with a child later eating more of that food when it becomes available and with a reduced ability to stop eating when full — though even here, some researchers note the reverse may also be true: a parent may restrict a food because a child already tends to overeat it, not the other way around. Pressuring a child to eat is more mixed, and research suggests it can run in both directions over time: a fussy eater can prompt more parental pressure, and pressure can, in turn, reinforce fussiness. Using food as a reward shows a similar pattern. None of this means a single instance of pressure or a reward causes lasting harm — it suggests the overall pattern of a mealtime relationship matters more than any one meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated carrot mixed through, served with milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated carrot mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with masoor dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe strawberry, hulled and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and thoroughly cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and thoroughly cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What this looks like at your table", "notes": "In practice, the parent''s side of the division looks fairly ordinary: family meals and snacks at broadly predictable times, while responding reasonably when a child shows genuine hunger. Water should remain freely available, and breastfeeding, illness, travel, growth needs and the family''s circumstances may require flexibility. Include a familiar, usually accepted food alongside something newer or less preferred. Parents do not need to become short-order cooks, but offering a simple familiar backup when appropriate is not a failure or a violation of responsive feeding. The same food is offered to the whole family, adjusted only for a toddler''s chewing ability. None of this requires a toddler to eat a specific amount, or to eat every food on the plate.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a small portion of mild, fully cooked minced mutton, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Rice with mixed dal and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with mixed dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pineapple, core removed and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chironji (charoli) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chironji has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why appetite changes from day to day, and why that''s normal", "notes": "A toddler''s appetite commonly varies from meal to meal and from day to day, particularly after growth slows following infancy. This variation is usually reassuring when the child remains active, eats from a reasonable range over time and continues to grow along their expected pattern. Persistent poor intake, weight loss, faltering growth, pain or difficulty eating or drinking should not be dismissed as an ordinary appetite fluctuation. Pressure, bargaining and repeated comments about quantity can make meals more stressful and may shift attention away from hunger and fullness cues.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with finely mashed pumpkin mixed through, served with plain curd or sambar", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with finely mashed pumpkin mixed through and a small portion of fully cooked, lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with moong dal and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon, seeds and rind removed and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chia seed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if chia seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The exception: when feeding itself is a medical concern", "notes": "This approach is designed for a typically developing toddler with ordinary appetite ups and downs — not a substitute for individual guidance when feeding itself is a diagnosed medical concern. A recognised condition called paediatric feeding disorder, along with avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID) — a diagnosable eating disorder, not merely a related concern — a documented growth or weight concern, or a significant sensory or developmental feeding difficulty, calls for assessment and an individualised plan from your child''s paediatrician, a registered dietitian, or an appropriately qualified feeding professional, such as a speech and language/swallowing therapist or occupational therapist working within a paediatric feeding team, rather than general reassurance. Seek prompt healthcare advice if your child regularly coughs, chokes, gags or appears distressed while eating; has pain, repeated vomiting or difficulty swallowing; takes unusually long to finish meals; loses weight or drops across their usual growth pattern; becomes dehydrated or lethargic; relies on a very narrow range of foods or nutritional drinks; or if feeding difficulties are significantly disrupting the child''s or family''s daily life. Sudden inability to swallow or breathe is an emergency. If you''re genuinely worried about your toddler''s growth, weight or feeding — not just today''s appetite dip — that''s a conversation for their healthcare team, not something to resolve from a general framework.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with grated beetroot mixed through, served with plain curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with grated beetroot mixed through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe litchi, skin and seed removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted makhana (fox nut) mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "dinner_a": "Rice with arhar dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Small habits that support this approach", "notes": "A few small habits support this approach without turning it into a project: keep the table itself free of the day''s report card — no counting bites out loud, no announcing how proud you are of a clean plate, no bargaining a toy or a screen for ''two more bites.'' If your family serves dessert, avoid using it as payment for finishing another food. It may be offered neutrally in a predictable way, without making it compulsory or giving it special moral value. Calm shared meals and supportive adult modelling can help, but families should not feel they have failed when work, fatigue or circumstances make eating together difficult.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated bottle gourd mixed through, served with a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated bottle gourd mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and soft-cooked raw banana (kachcha kela), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked raw banana (kachcha kela), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe dragon fruit, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground flaxseed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if flaxseed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with moong dal and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together", "notes": "The division of responsibility isn''t a technique that fixes a difficult mealtime overnight — it''s a way of thinking about whose job is whose, so a parent can stop carrying a job that was never really theirs to control: how much someone else''s body wants to eat. For most healthy toddlers, the caregiver continues to offer adequate, varied food at suitable meals and snacks, while the child is allowed to respond to hunger and fullness without pressure. Parents still observe growth, development, feeding skills and the overall pattern over time.", "breakfast_a": "Soft appam with mashed banana mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Soft appam with mashed banana mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe persimmon, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground melon seed (magaz) mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if melon seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more from the foods available at the meal. If the meal has ended, offer the next planned meal or nourishing snack at an appropriate time; do not use hunger or delayed food as a punishment. Keep water available. Thinking back over this week''s meals, was there a moment you found yourself taking on your toddler''s job — deciding how much they should eat — rather than your own?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You may not need to change anything about what you cook this week. Instead, notice which job you''re doing at each meal — offering the food, or trying to manage how much of it gets eaten. If your toddler eats only a very limited range of foods, or you''re concerned about growth or feeding, discuss the overall pattern with their healthcare team.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
