-- postpartum week 141: Eating well while travelling with a baby or toddler
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   141,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while travelling with a baby or toddler',
   '"Travel days genuinely disrupt routine — but a few portable, non-perishable staples packed ahead of time mean a delayed train or a long drive doesn''t have to become a day of skipped meals."',
   'Travelling with a baby or toddler — whether it''s a short trip to family or something longer — tends to disrupt routine more than almost anything else, and your own meals are often the first casualty of a packed itinerary or a delayed connection. It''s easy to end up running on packaged snacks and whatever''s available at the last minute. A little planning ahead — a few portable staples, a sense of what''s safe to eat once you arrive, and permission to eat at odd times if the schedule demands it — can keep travel days from becoming days where you barely eat.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing, especially if a travel schedule shifts your usual medication timing. If you''re travelling somewhere with limited access to food you''d normally eat: carrying a few days'' worth of any prescribed supplements, and checking with your care team ahead of a longer trip, is worth doing before you leave. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Bengali home cooking (shukto, jhol, ghonto, chorchori, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Travel with a young child rarely goes exactly to plan, and mealtimes are often the first thing to slip. Packing a few sturdy, non-perishable items — roasted chana, dry fruit, whole fruit, crackers — before you leave means there''s something reliable to eat even if a planned stop gets missed.", "breakfast_a": "Luchi, deep-fried flatbread (2 pieces) with an aloo dum and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Shukto, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a shukto (1 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage ghonto", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Packing food for transit", "notes": "For a train, flight or long drive, plan around when you''ll actually be able to eat safely. Meals eaten just before you leave, or after you arrive, can be the same as any other day — freshly made, including dairy, egg or fish dishes. For food eaten mid-journey, stick to something that doesn''t need chilling: roasted nuts and seeds, dry fruit, whole fruit that doesn''t bruise easily, plain crackers, or another shelf-stable snack. A flask of water and a reusable bottle you can refill are worth carrying regardless of what else you pack. Keeping packed food safe: The dishes above (curd, fish curry, cut fruit) are meant to be eaten fresh before you leave or after you arrive, not carried unrefrigerated for hours. If you do need to carry any of them mid-journey, keep them properly chilled with a frozen cold pack in an insulated bag, or safely hot in a sealed, insulated container — don''t leave them at room temperature. If you can''t keep food properly chilled or hot, choose the shelf-stable option below instead, or buy a freshly cooked meal once you arrive. Discard anything that''s been at room temperature for more than two hours, or one hour above 32°C. A transit-friendly option, no kitchen needed: Roasted chana or peanuts (30g), a banana or another sturdy whole fruit, and plain crackers or dry roti (2-3 pieces) — shelf-stable, no chilling or reheating needed, and easy to eat one-handed.", "breakfast_a": "Chirer pulao, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Peanut butter toast (2 slices, 1 tablespoon peanut butter) with a glass of milk (200ml) and a banana", "lunch_a": "Cholar dal, Bengal gram dal with coconut (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Bengali fish curry, home-packed (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans chorchori", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut apple", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach ghonto", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Bengali-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Navigating unfamiliar food away from home", "notes": "Eating away from your usual kitchen doesn''t mean eating worse — most towns and cities have some version of dal, rice, roti and a cooked vegetable available, which covers the basics even when nothing else about the trip is familiar. Sticking to freshly cooked, hot food and reliable water sources is the main precaution worth keeping in mind. Worth knowing: In an unfamiliar place, freshly cooked hot food from a reasonably busy, visibly clean place is generally a safer bet than something that''s been sitting out, and bottled or reliably treated water is worth prioritising over tap water you''re unsure of, especially while breastfeeding.", "breakfast_a": "Luchi with cholar dal (2 pieces, 1 cup dal) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a shukto (1 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower chorchori", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot ghonto", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Keeping meals roughly regular despite a disrupted schedule", "notes": "Travel days often mean meals happen later, earlier, or out of order compared to your usual pattern — that''s fine occasionally, but going long stretches without eating because the timing is off can leave you running on empty by evening. A packed snack eaten at the \"wrong\" time is still better than waiting until you''re overly hungry to eat anything at all.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Cholar dal with lauki (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Bengali-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi chorchori", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Urad dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked urad dal, plus lauki) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Breastfeeding or expressing on the move", "notes": "If you''re breastfeeding or carrying expressed milk while travelling, staying hydrated and eating regularly both matter more, not less, on a demanding travel day. Carrying a refillable water bottle and a couple of protein-containing snacks alongside your usual feeding supplies is worth building into your packing routine.", "breakfast_a": "Chirer pulao with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Shukto with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Bengali fish jhol (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower ghonto", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Paneer-and-vegetable curry, Bengali-style (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a beans chorchori", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Bengali-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Coming home and settling back into your usual pattern", "notes": "After a trip, it can take a day or two to feel back on your usual eating rhythm, and that''s completely normal. Restocking your kitchen with a few staples — dal, rice, curd, eggs — before you''re too tired to think about it makes that first day home easier than arriving to an empty fridge.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a shukto (1 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage chorchori", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots and peas) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Bengali-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach chorchori", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What would you pack differently next time you travel, based on what worked or didn''t this week?", "breakfast_a": "Luchi, deep-fried flatbread (2 pieces) with an aloo dum and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Shukto, mixed vegetable stew (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a shukto (1 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage ghonto", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage ghonto", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 142: Your own eating as breastfeeding frequency changes
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   142,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your own eating as breastfeeding frequency changes',
   '"As nursing becomes less frequent, your appetite and calorie needs may shift too — that''s a normal adjustment, not something to manage anxiously, and the calcium and protein habits worth keeping are worth keeping regardless of how often you''re nursing."',
   'If breastfeeding is becoming less frequent in your household — whether that''s a deliberate choice, your child''s own lead, or simply how things are settling by this stage — your own appetite and calorie needs may shift along with it. This is a normal, expected adjustment, not something that needs managing anxiously or correcting. This week is about letting your eating settle to match your actual hunger during this transition, while keeping the calcium and protein habits that were serving you well, since those matter for your own long-term health well beyond how often you''re nursing. This week is here for whenever it''s relevant to you — if you''re not currently changing how often you breastfeed, feel free to skip ahead; nothing here assumes every mother''s journey looks the same.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If an appetite change feels severe, persistent, or comes with other symptoms that worry you: mention it to your healthcare team rather than assuming it will simply pass on its own. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Odia home cooking (dalma, santula, besara, machha jhola, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "As breastfeeding frequency reduces — whether gradually or by your child''s own choice — your appetite may change too: for some mothers it eases off, for others it stays much the same. That''s a normal adjustment either way, not a sign anything is wrong, and it''s fine to let your intake settle to match your actual hunger rather than eating to a fixed target from earlier months.", "breakfast_a": "Poda pitha, fermented rice cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma, dal with vegetables (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a dalma (1 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage santula", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Keeping the habits that were serving you well", "notes": "If nursing has been prompting you to keep a glass of milk or a bowl of curd within reach, or to eat a bit more regularly through the day, those habits are worth keeping even as feeding frequency drops — they were good for you independently of how much milk your body was making.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Besara, mustard-based vegetable curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Odia fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a besara vegetable side", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Toor dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Odia-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Appetite changes don''t need to be managed anxiously", "notes": "Your appetite may change as nursing frequency drops — for some mothers it eases off, for others it barely changes. Trusting whatever your own signals are telling you, rather than forcing a fixed intake out of habit, is reasonable — the target is eating enough to match your own body, not replicating an earlier phase''s numbers. Worth knowing: Appetite changes as nursing reduces are common and not usually a cause for concern on their own. If an appetite change is severe, persistent, or comes with other symptoms that worry you, it''s worth mentioning to your healthcare team rather than assuming it will simply pass.", "breakfast_a": "Poda pitha with jaggery (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a dalma (1 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach santula", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Calcium habits stay worth keeping either way", "notes": "Calcium remains important as breastfeeding changes. Keep dependable calcium sources in your meals — a dependable calcium habit built now, through curd, milk or paneer, is a good one to keep for your own bone health well beyond this phase, not just for as long as you''re nursing.", "breakfast_a": "Freshly cooked rice mixed with curd, made with rice cooled quickly and refrigerated before mixing (1 cup) with a small portion of paneer (50g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma with lauki (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Odia-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki santula", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "No need to abruptly overhaul how you eat", "notes": "It''s tempting to treat a change in feeding frequency as a cue to suddenly change your diet — cutting portions sharply, for instance. There''s no need to overhaul anything abruptly; a gradual, appetite-led adjustment over the coming weeks is a reasonable, comfortable pace.", "breakfast_a": "Poda pitha with grated coconut (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Besara with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Odia machha jhola (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Paneer-stuffed paratha (2 pieces, includes a measured 100g paneer filling) with a small bowl of curd (150g) and a cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Odia-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across this transition matters, not one day''s appetite", "notes": "Some days appetite may feel almost like before, other days noticeably less — both are a normal part of this transition, and neither needs to be corrected in the moment. What matters over these weeks is that your overall eating stays varied and includes protein and calcium regularly, whatever the day-to-day appetite looks like.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a dalma (1 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower santula", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots and peas) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Odia-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Has your appetite noticeably shifted as feeding frequency has changed, and how has that felt to adjust to?", "breakfast_a": "Poda pitha, fermented rice cake (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalma, dal with vegetables (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a dalma (1 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage santula", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Odia-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage santula", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 143: Eating gently through a genuinely hard stretch
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   143,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating gently through a genuinely hard stretch',
   '"Ordinary, adjustable meals are still here for the days appetite is fine — and for the days it isn''t, each day also offers a gentler starting point: smaller amounts, more often, with simple additions to keep some nourishment going."',
   'A loss, a difficult diagnosis affecting someone close to you, or another genuinely hard stretch can make food feel unimportant, or blunt appetite noticeably — for some mothers, on some days. It doesn''t happen the same way for everyone, and there''s no need to assume every day this week will look like that. This week''s meals are the same ordinary, adjustable portions as any other week — the default here is exactly what it would be otherwise. Alongside them, each day also offers a separate "if your appetite is low" adaptation: smaller amounts eaten more often, with simple additions like nut butter, paneer or a boiled egg to help keep some protein and energy going, rather than just eating less across the board. Use whichever fits a given day — there''s no need to follow the same approach all week, and no need to use the low-appetite option at all if it doesn''t apply to you.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your prescribed medication plan. If your food intake drops significantly, contact your care team promptly for medicine-specific advice — don''t wait to see if it passes, and don''t independently stop or change your medicine. If you use insulin, eating less does not mean stopping or skipping a dose; any adjustment needs to follow your own individual plan, not a general rule. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another daily medication: follow your prescribed medication plan. If intake drops significantly, contact your pharmacist or care team promptly for medicine-specific advice — don''t independently stop or change your medicine, even on days when eating is difficult. If appetite loss continues for more than a couple of weeks, or comes with significant weight loss: arrange a medical review. A counsellor can offer valuable support through grief, but doesn''t replace an assessment of reduced intake or unexplained weight loss. Seek medical help promptly, without waiting: if you''re unable to keep fluids down, or you feel dehydrated, faint, or confused — these need attention sooner than a routine appetite check-in. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Himachali/Pahari home cooking (siddu, madra, chana madra, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical. If your appetite is low on a particular day, each day below also offers a separate adaptation: smaller amounts eaten more often through the day, with a few simple additions — a spoon of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, plain curd, or a boiled egg, depending on what suits your diet — to help keep some protein and energy going rather than simply eating less across the board. A banana or a glass of milk is a reasonable thing to start with on a day like that, not a complete meal on its own; build on it as you''re able to.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A loss, a hard diagnosis in the family, or another genuinely difficult stretch can blunt appetite for a while — food may feel unimportant, or simply hard to face, on some days more than others. That''s a normal, temporary response to real distress, not something wrong with you. The meals below are the same as any other week; if a full plate feels like too much on a particular day, a gentler adaptation is offered too, further down each day. If your appetite is low today Start with something small — a banana or a small glass of milk is a reasonable thing to begin with, not a full meal on its own — and add nourishment as you''re able: a spoonful of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, or a boiled egg stirred into a few spoonfuls of the khichdi. Eating in smaller amounts more often, rather than three full meals, is a reasonable way through a day like this. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A small bowl of curd, whenever it''s manageable.", "breakfast_a": "Siddu, steamed wheat bun (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana madra, chickpeas in a yoghurt gravy (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chickpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Pahari-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi, rice and moong dal (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus lauki) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with a khichdi, rice and moong dal (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus lauki)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Small and frequent is one option, not the only one", "notes": "If a full plate feels like too much on a given day, smaller amounts spaced more often can be easier to manage than three complete meals — that''s what the \"if your appetite is low\" option below is for. The meals above stay the same regular portions as any other week, for whenever they suit you. If your appetite is low today Eating in smaller amounts, more often through the day, can be easier to manage than three full meals — a few spoonfuls of dal and rice, or a banana or a small glass of milk to start with. You don''t need to force a large meal if one doesn''t appeal; try another small eating opportunity later instead, adding a spoon of nut butter or a small portion of paneer when you can, to help keep some protein going. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A banana or a small glass of milk, with a spoon of nut butter if that''s manageable.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Madra, vegetables in a yoghurt gravy (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Pahari trout, pan-cooked (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small cup of buttermilk (150ml) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When to mention it to your healthcare team", "notes": "Most appetite loss after a hard event eases gradually on its own. If it''s stretching on for weeks, or you''re finding it hard to eat almost anything at all, that''s worth raising with a doctor or counsellor rather than waiting it out indefinitely. Your own hydration, strength and health matter, whether or not you are breastfeeding. Worth knowing: A loss of appetite lasting more than a couple of weeks, or with significant weight loss, is worth arranging a medical review for — a counsellor can offer valuable support through grief, but doesn''t replace an assessment of reduced intake or unexplained weight loss. This is different from urgent symptoms: if you''re unable to keep fluids down, or you feel dehydrated, faint, or confused, seek medical help promptly rather than waiting. If your appetite is low today If food feels hard to face today, small, frequent amounts are a reasonable approach — a few spoonfuls at a time, spaced through the day, rather than aiming for full meals. Curd, milk, or a boiled egg with a little toast are all easy additions when you can manage them. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A small bowl of curd, on its own, whenever it''s manageable.", "breakfast_a": "Siddu with ghee (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a madra vegetable side", "lunch_b": "Pahari-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Letting someone else help with food, if that''s available", "notes": "If family or friends are offering to cook or bring food during a hard stretch, accepting that help is a reasonable, practical choice, not a burden you''re placing on them. It''s one less decision to make on a day when even small decisions can feel like a lot. If your appetite is low today Accepting help with food on a day like this is a practical choice, not a burden on anyone. If a full plate still feels like too much, a few spoonfuls at a time — with a spoon of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, or a boiled egg added in when you can — keeps some protein going without needing to finish a whole plate. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: Whatever''s easiest, whenever it''s manageable — even a few bites counts.", "breakfast_a": "Whatever''s offered or easiest today is completely fine — or, as a default, suji upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Whatever''s offered or easiest today is completely fine — or, as a default, boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana madra (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chickpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pahari trout (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g), with a pinch of roasted cumin if that helps", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Pahari-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with a phulka (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Gently returning toward your usual pattern, when you''re ready", "notes": "There''s no fixed timeline for appetite to return to normal after a hard stretch — for some it''s days, for others longer, and that''s fine either way. When you do notice hunger coming back, easing toward your usual meals rather than a full return all at once tends to feel more manageable. If your appetite is low today There''s no fixed timeline for appetite to return — if today still feels like a low-appetite day, smaller amounts more often, with a spoon of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, or a boiled egg added in, are still a reasonable way through it. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A small bowl of curd, whenever it''s manageable.", "breakfast_a": "Siddu (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Madra (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Pahari-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a piece of seasonal fruit", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with rice (1 cup cooked) and a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Some days will still be harder than others", "notes": "Even as things gradually feel more manageable, some days — an anniversary, a reminder, an unexpectedly hard moment — may bring appetite loss back briefly. That''s a normal part of grief and doesn''t mean you''ve lost the progress you''ve made. The same gentle approach still works on those days. If your appetite is low today Some days — an anniversary, a reminder, an unexpectedly hard moment — bring appetite loss back briefly, even after things have felt more manageable. The same gentle approach still works: smaller amounts, more often, with a spoon of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, or a boiled egg added in when you can. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A small bowl of curd, whenever it''s manageable.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a madra vegetable side", "lunch_b": "Pahari-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-rice khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with rice (1 cup cooked) and a rajma (3/4 cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or whatever felt most manageable earlier in the week. If your appetite is low today Start with something small — a banana or a small glass of milk is a reasonable thing to begin with, not a full meal on its own — and add nourishment as you''re able: a spoonful of nut butter, a small portion of paneer, or a boiled egg stirred into a few spoonfuls of the khichdi. Eating in smaller amounts more often, rather than three full meals, is a reasonable way through a day like this. Another small eating opportunity, if manageable: A small bowl of curd, whenever it''s manageable. Is there someone — family, a friend, your healthcare team — you could let help with food or check in with, if this week has been genuinely hard?", "breakfast_a": "Siddu, steamed wheat bun (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana madra, chickpeas in a yoghurt gravy (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chickpeas) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Pahari-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi, rice and moong dal (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus lauki) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with a khichdi, rice and moong dal (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus lauki)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'If this week wasn''t a hard one, is there someone in your life going through a difficult stretch who this gentle, low-pressure approach to eating might help, even just to share?',
   'The next set of weeks returns to this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 144: Taking stock — building your own simple, personal usual week
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   144,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Taking stock — building your own simple, personal usual week',
   '"Not every habit from this series needed to stick, and that''s fine — what''s worth keeping now is whatever actually fits your life, turned into your own simple, personal routine rather than something you have to keep following."',
   'This series has now covered a wide range of weekly topics — from the earliest days of recovery through toddlerhood and the everyday realities of feeding a household. This week is deliberately different: a chance to look back at what''s actually stuck, rather than take on something new. Not every suggestion from every week was ever meant to become a permanent habit — some were seasonal, some simply didn''t fit, and some quietly became part of how you eat without you noticing. This week is about noticing that, and building your own simple, personal sense of what a normal week of eating looks like for you now.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: keep following your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing going forward — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements — check in with your pharmacist if anything about that routine changes. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Malvani/coastal Maharashtrian home cooking (solkadhi, kalvan, usal, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This series has covered a lot of ground — this week is a chance to look back rather than learn something new. Some habits from earlier weeks probably stuck without much effort; others may never have fit your life, and that''s completely fine. Neither outcome reflects on how well you''ve been doing.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked), an usal (1 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken kalvan, coconut-based chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Which habits actually stuck, without effort?", "notes": "Think back over the past weeks — is there a food or a small routine that''s just become part of how you eat now, without you having to remind yourself? Those effortless habits are usually the ones worth building your ongoing routine around, since they''ve already proven they fit.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kalvan, coconut-based vegetable curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Malvani fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Malvani-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Letting go of what didn''t fit, without guilt", "notes": "Some things this series suggested probably didn''t stick, whether because they didn''t suit your taste, your schedule, or your household. That''s useful information, not a shortcoming — it simply tells you what to leave out of your own ongoing routine going forward. Worth knowing: If a particular habit from this series never worked for your life — a food you don''t enjoy, a routine your schedule can''t support — letting it go isn''t a failure. The goal was always to find what''s sustainable for you, not to adopt every suggestion.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna with jaggery (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a kalvan vegetable side", "lunch_b": "Chicken kalvan (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "The two or three helpful habits worth keeping", "notes": "Out of everything this series has covered, most people find just two or three habits genuinely worth protecting long-term — a dependable calcium source most days, eating something before you''re overly hungry, or not skipping meals entirely on busy days, for instance. Naming your own two or three, specifically, makes them easier to actually keep.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Malvani-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken kalvan (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A simple ''usual week'' template, in your own words", "notes": "Rather than a new theme to follow, try sketching what an ordinary week of eating now actually looks like for you — your real breakfasts, your real lunches, the snacks you reach for. That''s your own baseline going forward, built from what''s actually worked, not a list to aspire to.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna with grated coconut (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kalvan with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Malvani fish fry with a light curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Paneer bhurji, scrambled paneer with vegetables (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Malvani-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Trusting yourself going forward", "notes": "By now you likely know your own appetite, your own schedule, and your own household''s needs better than any weekly theme could account for. That knowledge is the real outcome of this series — not perfect adherence to any single week''s suggestions, but a working sense of what keeps you fed well, most of the time.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with grated carrot (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a kalvan vegetable side", "lunch_b": "Chicken kalvan (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Moong dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, plus carrots and peas) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Malvani-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or better yet, whatever your own ''usual week'' looks like by now. Looking back across this whole series, what''s the one habit you''d tell another mother to keep, if she only had time for one?", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked), an usal (1 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken kalvan, coconut-based chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Your own routine, built over these past weeks, is now the foundation to return to, wherever this series continues next within Phase 5 of 6.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 145: Herbal teas, galactagogues and everyday supplements — what's worth knowing
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   145,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Herbal teas, galactagogues and everyday supplements — what''s worth knowing',
   '"Frequent, effective milk removal is the one thing reliably shown to support milk supply — no tea, powder or capsule has that same evidence behind it, however it''s marketed. Eating well supports your own health throughout, but it isn''t a substitute for that."',
   'If you''re currently breastfeeding, herbal teas and "lactation" supplements are everywhere — recommended by relatives, sold at pharmacies, promoted online. It''s worth knowing what''s actually backed by evidence, what''s traditional but unproven, and what''s worth a genuine safety check before you start. This isn''t about telling you to avoid these products altogether. But using a spice in cooking is different from taking it regularly as a concentrated lactation remedy — safety then depends on the specific ingredient, preparation, amount and any medicine you take, and a herbal tea isn''t automatically exempt from that just because it''s called a tea. It''s about making an informed, ingredient-by-ingredient choice rather than an assumed one, and knowing which single change (frequent, effective milk removal) actually has the strongest evidence behind it if supply is a genuine concern.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: keep following your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — and if you''re considering fenugreek specifically, check with them first, since it can lower blood glucose further alongside your medication. The A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine or another ongoing medication: keep following your prescribed medication timing around meals and calcium or iron supplements, and check with your pharmacist before adding any herbal or ayurvedic supplement — some can interact with thyroid medication or its absorption. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Arunachali home cooking (zan, bamboo shoot preparations, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical. Fresh bamboo shoots contain compounds that must be removed by proper preparation before eating — use commercially prepared, ready-to-cook bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions, or make sure home-prepared shoots are peeled, sliced thin, and boiled in fresh changes of water (or fermented) and fully cooked before serving.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re currently breastfeeding, you''ve probably come across herbal teas, powders or capsules marketed to increase milk supply — often sold as \"lactation\" products. If you''re not currently breastfeeding, the supply-related framing this week doesn''t apply to you, but the general caution about supplement quality and checking with your own doctor before starting anything new still does. Worth knowing: Most commercially marketed \"lactation\" teas and supplements haven''t been rigorously tested for effectiveness or safety while breastfeeding. Check with your lactation consultant or doctor before starting one regularly, especially if you take any other medication.", "breakfast_a": "Zan, a ragi (finger millet) porridge (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Ngatok, Arunachali-style lemongrass fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Fenugreek — the most common one, and what it actually does", "notes": "Fenugreek (methi) is probably the single most widely used galactagogue. The research on whether it meaningfully increases milk supply is mixed and generally weak — some small studies show an effect, others show none, and reviews have not found consistent, reliable evidence either way. A harmless side effect worth knowing about in advance: it can give sweat, urine or milk a maple-syrup-like smell, which is normal and not a sign anything is wrong. Fenugreek is a legume, so it can also cause digestive upset (bloating, loose stools) or an allergic reaction in some people, particularly if you have a peanut, chickpea or other legume allergy — its safety profile isn''t limited to the blood-glucose effect below. Worth knowing: If you manage diabetes, prediabetes or take any blood-glucose-lowering medication, talk to your care team before using fenugreek regularly — it can lower blood glucose further, on top of your medication.", "breakfast_a": "Chura sabzi, flattened rice with vegetables, ginger and garlic (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao, spiced rice with mixed vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A handful of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What''s actually well-supported — feeding pattern and steady eating", "notes": "The one intervention consistently shown to support milk supply is frequent, effective milk removal — by your baby or a pump — not a tea, a powder, or a change to what you eat. Eating enough and staying hydrated matter for your own health throughout, but they''re supportive habits, not an established supply fix, so this isn''t a suggestion that low supply means you simply need to eat more. If supply is a genuine concern, that''s worth raising directly with a lactation consultant, who can check positioning, frequency and effectiveness of removal — the modifiable factor with real evidence behind it.", "breakfast_a": "Til pitha, sesame-and-rice flour pancakes (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela, savoury lentil pancakes with a fried egg (2 pieces, 1 egg) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Ngatok, Arunachali-style lemongrass fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A piece of seasonal fruit"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Reading a label — \"lactation supplement\" isn''t a standardised category", "notes": "Many herbal or ayurvedic products sold for breastfeeding mothers aren''t held to the same quality or dosing standards as regulated medicines — the labelled ingredients and amounts aren''t always independently verified, and \"natural\" doesn''t automatically mean safe while breastfeeding. Where possible, choose products from brands that third-party test their supplements, check the full ingredient list rather than just the front-of-pack claim, and mention anything you''re taking regularly to your doctor or lactation consultant. Third-party testing can confirm a product actually contains what its label states, at the stated amount — it doesn''t by itself establish that the product is safe or effective to take while breastfeeding.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-vegetable pulao, spiced rice with kidney beans and vegetables (1 1/2 cups) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with a stuffed paratha (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Other common herbs — fennel, ajwain, jeera, shatavari", "notes": "Fennel (saunf), ajwain and jeera are common kitchen spices, and using them in your everyday cooking is a different thing from taking any of them regularly as a concentrated tea or a lactation remedy — once it''s a remedy rather than a seasoning, safety depends on the specific ingredient, preparation, amount and any medicine you take, and a herbal tea isn''t automatically exempt from that just because it''s called a tea. Shatavari is different again: it''s typically taken as a standalone supplement rather than an everyday spice, and the same check-with-your-doctor-first approach applies, particularly if you''re on regular medication. None of this means an occasional cup of fennel tea is inherently risky — it means this can''t guarantee safety just by calling something a tea.", "breakfast_a": "Poha, flattened rice with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Chana dal-and-vegetable khichdi (1 1/2 cups total, made with a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, plus carrots) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji, scrambled paneer with vegetables (100g paneer) with a phulka (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few dates (4-5) with a small handful of nuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "What to actually ask before starting anything new", "notes": "Before starting any tea, powder or capsule — herbal or otherwise — three questions are worth taking to your lactation consultant or doctor: is there any real evidence it helps, does it interact with any medication or condition you have (thyroid, diabetes, blood pressure), and is the specific product you''re considering from a source that checks its own quality. Those three questions apply just as much to a multivitamin or a protein powder as to a herbal tea.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya, broken wheat porridge with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha, a stuffed flatbread with spiced scrambled egg (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a brinjal sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Missi roti, a spiced gram-flour flatbread (3 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "Roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Is there a tea, powder or supplement you''ve been taking without really knowing why — and is it worth checking on at your next appointment?", "breakfast_a": "Zan, a ragi (finger millet) porridge (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Ngatok, Arunachali-style lemongrass fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot-and-vegetable sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
